      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "933123c3_e042a809",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000016
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-08-03T12:32:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Probably because a type-c adapter is recognized as a new power supply\n\u003e but it\u0027s not powerful enough to sustain the system\n\u003e (in the case of Pixel XL and the compliance test harness).\n\nNo, our power manager code is using the most powerful source, so it should not switch source or brown out in this case.\n\n\u003e \n\u003e We decided not to switch power source once the system is up \u0026 running. \n\u003e Fizz checks only once at boot whether the system is powered enough or not. \n\u003e We\u0027ll throttle the CPU to keep the power consumption below the threshold. \n\u003e I think we want to simplify power management. That\u0027s why we don\u0027t want to \n\u003e switch to even a higher power supply.\n\nThis is a terrible user experience, did the project management agree on this ?",
      "parentUuid": "f825ccd0_c479f0bc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 64
      },
      "revId": "a296ac6732eda35e869d0aa8ba6d20e33d71125f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a4b1feb9_982ec3e6",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 13,
      "author": {
        "id": 1106949
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-08-03T22:25:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e No, our power manager code is using the most powerful source, so it should \n\u003e not switch source or brown out in this case.\n\nI dug deeper. It browns out because PD\u0027s supplier priority is higher than BJ\u0027s priority. So, switch over happens no matter what power a type-c charger supplies (9.5W for Pixel XL).\n\nAnother thing I found is even if a new power is high (e.g. 45W), the system still browns out. I verified this using Zinger. I was talking at the project meeting that this dynamic power switch can be a feature added later by AU but it doesn\u0027t seem so. The next build will have no charger chip. So, I\u0027ll revisit this once I get the new hardware.",
      "parentUuid": "933123c3_e042a809",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 13,
        "startChar": 20,
        "endLine": 13,
        "endChar": 64
      },
